parts:
- text: 'I have read the full description of Research Task 1: Neural Activity Forecasting on ZAPBench. The task involves forecasting neural activity for a large number of neurons (71,721) over 32 future timesteps given 4 input timesteps, using MAE as the primary metric.


    To understand the training framework, the task description recommends reading `defaults.py`. This will help me understand how to structure my submission.


    `/execute_action{storage read system/defaults.py}`'
thinking_content: '**Understanding the ZAPBench Neural Activity Forecasting Task**


  Okay, so I''ve just been briefed on the details of Research Task 1 on ZAPBench. It''s all about forecasting the activity of a massive network of zebrafish neurons – 71,721 of them, to be exact. The core challenge is to predict the next 32 timesteps of activity, given only the preceding 4 timesteps as input.


  The primary metric here is Mean Absolute Error, averaged across those 32 output timesteps, all evaluated on a validation set. They''re also providing secondary MAE scores at specific timepoints which will be useful for a more granular understanding. The input data shape is (batch\_size, 4, 71721) and the output is (batch\_size, 32, 71721).


  The submission requirements are very specific, which is good to see as it streamlines the evaluation process. We need to implement Python functions for defining hyperparameters, creating the network, computing the loss, setting up the optimizer, and finalizing the results. They''re also giving us a `test` function to evaluate our performance.


  They''re running 3 parallel training runs with different seeds, and the final score will be the mean negative MAE. Early stopping is automatic, which is a nice touch.


  There are some clear constraints that I need to keep in mind: no pretrained models, no custom training loops, and a 24GB GPU memory limit.


  It''s also interesting to see the baseline provided, the "Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline". It''s pretty straightforward, treating each neuron as independent and identical, which is a significant limitation I can exploit. The system''s design will hopefully make it easier to go beyond such a baseline approach.


  My next step is definitely to dive into `defaults.py`. I need to get a clear picture of the training framework to know how the functions I have to implement are integrated into the pipeline. That''ll provide the context I need to design an efficient and effective solution.

  '
