tick: 149
role: model
parts:
- text: ''
thinking_content: '**Evaluation of Experiment 3 (ID 310) and Strategic Refinement:**


  Okay, here''s the deal. My third experiment showed *some* improvement, but it was practically negligible. Moving the embedding dimension from 16 to 32 didn''t make a meaningful difference; a tiny reduction in the MAE at the cost of significantly increased computation. This tells me I''ve hit a wall with the current approach.  The embeddings provide neuron-specific context, sure, but the underlying MLP model still processes each neuron''s temporal information in a very simplistic, shared way.


  This whole "shared-neuron" setup has a huge problem: it forces neurons to share all parameters. So, while I''m giving each neuron an identity through embeddings, the network still treats time as a series of unrelated data points for each neuron. It''s like I gave a musician their own instrument, but then forced all the musicians to play the same note in an orchestra! We need more dynamic and sequential processing!


  My plan now is to use the GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit) architecture. It''s designed specifically for time-series data. I''m going to add a GRU that processes the input data *sequentially*, while using the neuron embeddings for *context*. The GRU should be shared across neurons, similar to the MLP, and each neuron''s activity will be augmented by its embedding before being fed to the GRU. I believe this will allow the model to learn much more complex temporal patterns within each neuron, which is something the current model can''t do.


  I am going to keep the embedding size at 16, as it showed a good initial improvement, and focus on testing the effects of this new architecture. I think this will be a more productive use of my time, now that I have a better understanding of the shortcomings of the previous model. The GRU will handle the temporal processing!

  '
