parts:
- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will now add a new entry to my research log (Capsule 1) detailing the results of my third (ID 310) and fourth (ID 324) experiments, my analysis of the negative result from the global context, and the refined plan for the \"Per-Timestep Mean Global Context\" experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Update: Global Context Attempt Failed, Refining Approach\"\ncontent: |\n  **Station Tick:** 156\n  **Agent Name:** Lumina I\n  \n  **1. Experiment 3: Shared-Neuron MLP with Neuron Embeddings (ID 310, Dim 32)**\n  - **Objective:** Further investigate the impact of neuron identity embeddings by increasing `embedding_dim` from 16 to 32.\n  - **Results:**\n    - My Score: -0.02869630893900223\n    - Previous Submission (ID 293, Dim 16) Score: -0.028718182773716943\n    - **Marginal Improvement:** Only a very slight, almost negligible improvement. Per-timestep MAE metrics were essentially identical. This indicates diminishing returns from\
    \ simply increasing embedding dimension within the current architecture.\n  \n  **2. Experiment 4: Neuron Embeddings + Pooled Global Context (ID 324, Dim 16, Global 64)**\n  - **Objective:** Introduce a \"Pooled Global Context\" mechanism to provide inter-neuronal awareness. A single global context vector was derived from the entire `input_horizon * num_neurons` flattened input.\n  - **Results:**\n    - My Score: -0.029083139172769595\n    - Best Previous Score (ID 293): -0.028718\n    - **Negative Result:** This experiment performed worse than both the neuron-embedding-only model and the system baseline.\n    - **MAE Metrics Comparison (lower is better):**\n      | Metric     | Best (ID 293) | My Submission (ID 324) |\n      |------------|---------------|------------------------|\n      | Val_MAE_1  | 0.0196        | 0.0215                 |\n      | Val_MAE_4  | 0.0233        | 0.0246                 |\n      | Val_MAE_8  | 0.0266        | 0.0273                 |\n      | Val_MAE_16\
    \ | 0.0300        | 0.0303                 |\n      | Val_MAE_32 | 0.0319        | 0.0321                 |\n    - The global context as implemented was detrimental, worsening performance across all horizons.\n  \n  **3. Reflection and Next Steps: Refined Global Context**\n  - **Analysis of Failure (ID 324):** The approach of flattening the entire input `(batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons)` into a single `(batch_size, input_horizon * num_neurons)` vector and then projecting it to `global_context_dim` likely resulted in significant information loss and/or the introduction of noise. The global context was too crude and lacked temporal granularity.\n  - **Revised Plan: Per-Timestep Mean Global Context:** Instead of a single, highly compressed global context for the entire 4-timestep input, I will compute a *mean activity* across all neurons *for each individual input timestep*. This will yield a `(batch_size, input_horizon)` global context. This temporally resolved global context will\
    \ then be concatenated to each neuron's input (alongside its temporal input and neuron embedding).\n  - **Rationale:** This method preserves the temporal dimension of the global context, allowing the MLP to perceive how the overall brain state evolves over the input window. It avoids the drastic compression and potential information loss of the previous attempt while still providing inter-neuronal information.\n  - **Hyperparameters:**\n    - `embedding_dim`: 16 (reverted for economy, as 32 offered no clear benefit)\n    - `hidden_size`: 128\n    - `dropout_rate`: 0.1\n    - `global_context_features`: This will now be `input_horizon` (4), as it's the raw mean activity per timestep. There's no separate `global_context_dim` projection needed for this iteration.\n  - **Hypothesis:** A temporally resolved global context, derived via mean pooling, will provide a more informative signal to the neuron-specific predictors, leading to improved forecasting accuracy by capturing more meaningful\
    \ inter-neuronal dynamics.\n```"
