parts:
- text: "I am in the Research Counter. My previous experiment (ID 324) showed that a simple, highly compressed global context was detrimental. Based on my reflection, I will now implement a \"Per-Timestep Mean Global Context\" approach. This refined method aims to provide a more informative and temporally resolved global signal to each neuron's prediction, thereby better leveraging inter-neuronal activity patterns.\n\nHere's the plan for my fifth experiment:\n1.  **Architecture:** `PerTimestepGlobalContextMLP`.\n2.  **Hyperparameters:**\n    *   `learning_rate`: 0.001\n    *   `hidden_size`: 128\n    *   `dropout_rate`: 0.1\n    *   `embedding_dim`: 16 (reverted to the more efficient size)\n    *   The \"global context\" will now be the mean activity across all neurons for each of the 4 input timesteps, so its dimension will implicitly be `input_horizon` (4).\n3.  **Model Logic:**\n    *   Generate neuron embeddings as before.\n    *   Calculate the mean activity across all `num_neurons`\
    \ for each `input_horizon` timestep, resulting in a `(batch_size, input_horizon)` tensor.\n    *   This per-timestep mean global context will then be expanded and concatenated with each neuron's individual temporal input and its neuron embedding.\n    *   The combined features will then be flattened and fed into the shared MLP.\n\nThis approach provides a richer context by preserving temporal information, hopefully leading to improved performance.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Neuron Embeddings + Per-Timestep Global Mean Context (Dim 16)\"\ntags: \"neuron-embedding, global-context, mean-pooling, shared-weights, mlp, neuronal-forecasting\"\nabstract: \"This submission refines the global context approach by computing a per-timestep mean activity across all neurons, rather than a single compressed vector. This temporally resolved global context, along with a neuron's temporal input and embedding (dimension 16), is fed into a shared MLP. This aims to provide a more granular\
    \ understanding of overall brain state, enhancing inter-neuronal awareness and forecasting.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import time\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Tuple, Optional\n  \n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define hyperparameter values for the model.\n      Includes embedding_dim for neuron identity embeddings.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_size': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'embedding_dim': 16 # Using 16 as 32 showed diminishing returns\n      }\n  \n  \n  class PerTimestepGlobalContextMLP(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Shared-neuron MLP with per-neuron embeddings and per-timestep global mean context.\n      \"\"\"\n      num_neurons: int \n      hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      embedding_dim: int\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n  \n      @nn.compact\n      def\
    \ __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons = x.shape \n  \n          # 1. Generate neuron IDs and embeddings\n          neuron_ids = jnp.arange(self.num_neurons) # (num_neurons,)\n          neuron_embeddings = nn.Embed(\n              num_embeddings=self.num_neurons,\n              features=self.embedding_dim,\n              name=\"neuron_embed\" \n          )(neuron_ids) # (num_neurons, embedding_dim)\n          \n          # Expand embeddings to match batch_size for concatenation later\n          # (num_neurons, embedding_dim) -> (1, num_neurons, embedding_dim) -> (batch_size, num_neurons, embedding_dim)\n          neuron_embeddings_batch_expanded = jnp.tile(neuron_embeddings[jnp.newaxis, :, :], (batch_size, 1, 1))\n  \n          # 2. Compute Per-Timestep Mean Global Context\n          # Mean activity across all neurons for each timestep: (batch_size, input_horizon)\n          global_mean_activity_per_timestep = jnp.mean(x, axis=-1)\n\
    \          \n          # Expand this global context to be replicated for each neuron for concatenation\n          # (batch_size, input_horizon) -> (batch_size, 1, input_horizon) -> (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)\n          global_mean_context_tiled = jnp.tile(\n              jnp.expand_dims(global_mean_activity_per_timestep, axis=1), \n              (1, num_neurons, 1)\n          )\n  \n          # 3. Prepare x for concatenation with embeddings and global context\n          # Original x: (batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons)\n          # Transpose x to (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon) to align for concatenation\n          x_transposed = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1)) # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)\n          \n          # Concatenate temporal input, neuron embeddings, and per-timestep global context\n          # For each (batch_item, neuron), the features are: \n          # [neuron_temporal_input (input_horizon), embedding_dim, global_mean_context_per_timestep\
    \ (input_horizon)]\n          total_features_per_neuron_input = input_horizon + self.embedding_dim + input_horizon\n          x_with_all_features = jnp.concatenate([\n              x_transposed, # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)\n              neuron_embeddings_batch_expanded, # (batch_size, num_neurons, embedding_dim)\n              global_mean_context_tiled # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)\n          ], axis=-1) # Result: (batch_size, num_neurons, total_features_per_neuron_input)\n  \n          # 4. Reshape for shared MLP processing\n          # From (batch_size, num_neurons, total_features)\n          # To (batch_size * num_neurons, total_features)\n          x_flat_mlp_input = jnp.reshape(x_with_all_features, (batch_size * num_neurons, total_features_per_neuron_input))\n  \n          # 5. Shared MLP layers\n          x_flat_mlp_output = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(x_flat_mlp_input)\n          x_flat_mlp_output = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(x_flat_mlp_output)\n\
    \          x_flat_mlp_output = nn.relu(x_flat_mlp_output)\n          x_flat_mlp_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(x_flat_mlp_output)\n  \n          x_flat_mlp_output = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(x_flat_mlp_output) # Output size (32)\n  \n          # 6. Reshape back to (batch_size, output_horizon, num_neurons)\n          x_final_output = jnp.reshape(x_flat_mlp_output, (batch_size, num_neurons, self.output_horizon))\n          x_final_output = jnp.transpose(x_final_output, (0, 2, 1)) # (batch_size, 32, num_neurons)\n  \n          return x_final_output\n  \n  \n  class PerTimestepGlobalContextMLPWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wrapper to make Flax model compatible with the training system.\n      Handles BatchNorm state and Dropout RNG requirements.\n      \"\"\"\n  \n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any], num_neurons: int = 71721):\n          self.model = PerTimestepGlobalContextMLP(\n              num_neurons=num_neurons,\n           \
    \   hidden_size=hparams['hidden_size'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate'],\n              embedding_dim=hparams['embedding_dim']\n          )\n          self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = True\n  \n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout},\n              dummy_input,\n              training=True\n          )\n          return variables\n  \n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable is not None:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  mutable=mutable,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n              return output, updates\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(\n\
    \                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n  \n  \n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the PerTimestepGlobalContextMLP network.\"\"\"\n      return PerTimestepGlobalContextMLPWrapper(hparams, num_neurons=71721)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Per-Timestep Mean Global Context Implementation Plan**\n\nOkay, I'm at the Research Counter, and it's time to submit this new experiment. Based on my previous work, this new model, which I'm calling `PerTimestepGlobalContextMLP`, will implement a \"Per-Timestep Mean Global Context\" approach.\n\nHere's the breakdown:\n\n1.  **Hyperparameters:** I'm sticking with a `learning_rate` of 0.001, `hidden_size` of 128, and a `dropout_rate` of 0.1, as these have consistently performed well. I'm reverting the `embedding_dim` to 16, as increasing it to 32 didn't yield a significant improvement. `input_horizon` is set at 4 and `num_neurons` is at 71721 as these values are given by the task.\n\n2.  **Model Structure:** The core idea is to enrich the input features for each neuron with a per-timestep global context.\n\n    *   **Neuron Embeddings:** These will be generated using `(num_neurons, embedding_dim)`.\n    *   **Per-Timestep Mean Global Context:**  The input `x` has shape\
  \ `(batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons)`.  I'll calculate the mean across the neurons for each timestep. This gives me a tensor `(batch_size, input_horizon)` which represents the per-timestep global context.  I'll then expand this context to match the shape of the input data and concatenate it.\n\nLet's refine the logic around that. For each neuron in each batch item, I want an input vector with these elements:\n    1.  The 4 timesteps of its own activity: `(input_horizon,)`.\n    2.  Its neuron embedding: `(embedding_dim,)`.\n    3.  The global mean activity across the 4 timesteps: `(input_horizon,)`\n\nSo, I need to concatenate along the last axis, for each neuron to be `(input_horizon + embedding_dim + input_horizon)`.\n\nHere's how I'll do the shape manipulations:\n\n*   The input `x` is `(batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons)`.\n*   Neuron embeddings are `(num_neurons, embedding_dim)`, and after expansion, `(batch_size, num_neurons, embedding_dim)`.\n*   The global mean context\
  \ calculation gets me `(batch_size, input_horizon)`. I need to reshape this to `(batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)` to make it work. I expand the dimension and tile the tensor to make it of the desired shape.\n*   I will transpose `x` to get `(batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)` and I also expand and tile the `global_mean_activity_per_timestep` to have shape `(batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)`\n*   Finally, I'll concatenate `x_transposed`, the expanded neuron embeddings, and the tiled global mean context, all along the last axis. This creates the combined feature vector.\n\nTo be precise, I have:\n1.  `(batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)` (neuron's own activity)\n2.  `(batch_size, num_neurons, embedding_dim)` (neuron embedding)\n3.  `(batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)` (tiled global context)\n\nConcatenating along the last axis gives `(batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon + embedding_dim + input_horizon)`. Then, I'll flatten to `(batch_size * num_neurons,\
  \ total_features)` before feeding it into the MLP.\n\nThis should work. I'm confident about this setup.\n"
