tick: 168
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Research Counter. My last experiment (ID 342) demonstrated that a learnable per-timestep global context significantly improved performance. I will now submit my seventh experiment, which aims to further enhance this by increasing the `global_context_projection_dim` from 32 to 64. This is to test if a more expressive global context can lead to additional performance gains.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Neuron Embeddings + Learnable Per-Timestep Global Context (Dim 16, Context 64)\"\ntags: \"neuron-embedding, global-context, learnable-context, hyperparameter-tuning, shared-weights, mlp, neuronal-forecasting\"\nabstract: \"This submission continues to refine the learnable global context approach by increasing the projection dimension from 32 to 64. For each input timestep, neuron activities are projected into a 64-dimensional global context vector, which is then concatenated with a neuron's temporal input and embedding (dimension 16) before being processed\
    \ by a shared MLP. This aims for a more detailed and effective global signal.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import time\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Tuple, Optional\n  \n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define hyperparameter values for the model.\n      Includes embedding_dim for neuron identity embeddings and global_context_projection_dim.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_size': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'embedding_dim': 16, # Using 16 as 32 showed diminishing returns\n          'global_context_projection_dim': 64 # Increased global context projection dimension\n      }\n  \n  \n  class LearnableGlobalContextMLP(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Shared-neuron MLP with per-neuron embeddings and a learnable per-timestep global context.\n      \"\"\"\n      num_neurons: int \n      hidden_size:\
    \ int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      embedding_dim: int\n      global_context_projection_dim: int # Dimension of the learnable global context per timestep\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n  \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons = x.shape \n  \n          # 1. Generate neuron IDs and embeddings\n          neuron_ids = jnp.arange(self.num_neurons) # (num_neurons,)\n          neuron_embeddings = nn.Embed(\n              num_embeddings=self.num_neurons,\n              features=self.embedding_dim,\n              name=\"neuron_embed\" \n          )(neuron_ids) # (num_neurons, embedding_dim)\n          \n          # Expand embeddings to match batch_size for concatenation later\n          # (num_neurons, embedding_dim) -> (1, num_neurons, embedding_dim) -> (batch_size, num_neurons, embedding_dim)\n          neuron_embeddings_batch_expanded = jnp.tile(neuron_embeddings[jnp.newaxis, :, :], (batch_size, 1, 1))\n\
    \  \n          # 2. Compute Learnable Per-Timestep Global Context\n          # Reshape X to (batch_size * input_horizon, num_neurons) for Dense layer\n          x_reshaped_for_global_proj = jnp.reshape(x, (batch_size * input_horizon, num_neurons))\n          \n          # Apply Dense layer to project global activity: (batch_size * input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n          projected_global_context_flat = nn.Dense(self.global_context_projection_dim, name=\"global_context_proj\")(x_reshaped_for_global_proj)\n          \n          # Reshape back to (batch_size, input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n          projected_global_context = jnp.reshape(projected_global_context_flat, \n                                                   (batch_size, input_horizon, self.global_context_projection_dim))\n          \n          # Tile across the neuron dimension, and ensure it's still per timestep\n          global_context_tiled = jnp.tile(\n              projected_global_context[:,\
    \ jnp.newaxis, :, :], # (batch_size, 1, input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n              (1, num_neurons, 1, 1) # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n          )\n  \n          # 3. Prepare x for concatenation with embeddings and global context\n          # Original x: (batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons)\n          # Transpose x to (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon) to align for concatenation\n          x_transposed = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1)) # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)\n          \n          # Reshape x_transposed to (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, 1)\n          x_transposed_expanded = jnp.expand_dims(x_transposed, axis=-1)\n          \n          # Expand neuron_embeddings for each timestep: (batch_size, num_neurons, 1, embedding_dim)\n          neuron_embeddings_timestep_expanded = jnp.tile(\n              neuron_embeddings_batch_expanded[:, :, jnp.newaxis, :],\n              (1, 1, input_horizon,\
    \ 1)\n          )\n          \n          # Concatenate temporal input, neuron embeddings, and learnable per-timestep global context\n          # For each (batch_item, neuron, timestep), the features are: \n          # [neuron_temporal_input_at_t (1), embedding_dim, global_context_projection_dim]\n          # Resulting shape before flattening for MLP:\n          # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, 1 + embedding_dim + global_context_projection_dim)\n          x_per_neuron_timestep_features = jnp.concatenate([\n              x_transposed_expanded, # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, 1)\n              neuron_embeddings_timestep_expanded, # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, embedding_dim)\n              global_context_tiled # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n          ], axis=-1)\n  \n          # Flatten the timestep dimension into the feature dimension per neuron for the shared MLP\n          # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon\
    \ * (1 + embedding_dim + global_context_projection_dim))\n          total_features_per_timestep = 1 + self.embedding_dim + self.global_context_projection_dim\n          total_features_per_neuron_input = input_horizon * total_features_per_timestep\n          \n          x_flat_mlp_input = jnp.reshape(x_per_neuron_timestep_features, \n                                         (batch_size * num_neurons, total_features_per_neuron_input))\n  \n          # 4. Shared MLP layers\n          x_flat_mlp_output = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(x_flat_mlp_input)\n          x_flat_mlp_output = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(x_flat_mlp_output)\n          x_flat_mlp_output = nn.relu(x_flat_mlp_output)\n          x_flat_mlp_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(x_flat_mlp_output)\n  \n          x_flat_mlp_output = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(x_flat_mlp_output) # Output size (32)\n  \n          # 5. Reshape back to (batch_size, output_horizon, num_neurons)\n\
    \          x_final_output = jnp.reshape(x_flat_mlp_output, (batch_size, num_neurons, self.output_horizon))\n          x_final_output = jnp.transpose(x_final_output, (0, 2, 1)) # (batch_size, 32, num_neurons)\n  \n          return x_final_output\n  \n  \n  class LearnableGlobalContextMLPWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wrapper to make Flax model compatible with the training system.\n      Handles BatchNorm state and Dropout RNG requirements.\n      \"\"\"\n  \n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any], num_neurons: int = 71721):\n          self.model = LearnableGlobalContextMLP(\n              num_neurons=num_neurons,\n              hidden_size=hparams['hidden_size'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate'],\n              embedding_dim=hparams['embedding_dim'],\n              global_context_projection_dim=hparams['global_context_projection_dim']\n          )\n          self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = True\n  \n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n\
    \          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout},\n              dummy_input,\n              training=True\n          )\n          return variables\n  \n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable is not None:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  mutable=mutable,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n              return output, updates\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n  \n  \n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the LearnableGlobalContextMLP network.\"\
    \"\"\n      return LearnableGlobalContextMLPWrapper(hparams, num_neurons=71721)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Iterating on Global Context: Bigger is Better?**


  Alright, here I am at the Research Counter again. Experiment ID 342, with that 32-dimensional per-timestep global context, showed some promising results. So, for my seventh experiment, I''m upping the ante. I''m bumping that `global_context_projection_dim` up to 64. My thinking is that a richer representation of the global state should, in theory, lead to better performance. It''s a key architectural component I''m focusing on, directly addressing those initial limitations we identified. Remember, the global context is all about capturing "information from other neurons," something the baseline was clearly lacking. The underlying code remains pretty much unchanged – just that one hyperparameter tweak. Let''s see if this jump to 64 pays off! This fits nicely with my current iterative research plan: identify the core issues, test a change, analyze the results. And on we go!

  '
