tick: 178
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Research Counter. I have reached maturity and can now see other agents' higher-scoring submissions. However, I will proceed with my planned experiment to introduce an RNN component for explicit temporal modeling, building on my own research path. This follows the guidance to \"refine promising approaches\" and allows me to establish a stronger internal baseline before deeply investigating external SOTA methods.\n\nMy ninth experiment will integrate a GRU for temporal modeling into my current architecture, which includes neuron embeddings (Dim 16) and a learnable per-timestep global context (Dim 32). The GRU's hidden size will be 64, and the final prediction layer will have a `hidden_size` of 128.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Neuron Embeddings + Learnable Global Context + GRU for Temporal Dynamics\"\ntags: \"neuron-embedding, global-context, learnable-context, rnn, gru, temporal-modeling, neuronal-forecasting\"\nabstract: \"This submission integrates\
    \ a GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit) for explicit temporal modeling within each neuron's prediction pathway. Building upon neuron embeddings (Dim 16) and a learnable per-timestep global context (Dim 32), the GRU processes the combined input over the 4 input timesteps. Its final hidden state is then passed to a Dense layer to predict the 32 output timesteps, aiming to better capture sequence dependencies.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import time\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Tuple, Optional\n  \n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define hyperparameter values for the model.\n      Includes embedding_dim for neuron identity embeddings, global_context_projection_dim,\n      and rnn_hidden_size for the GRU.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rnn_hidden_size': 64, # Hidden size for the GRU\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'embedding_dim':\
    \ 16, # Optimal from previous experiments\n          'global_context_projection_dim': 32 # Optimal from previous experiments\n      }\n  \n  \n  class GRUBasedForecaster(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      Shared-neuron GRU-based forecaster with per-neuron embeddings and a learnable per-timestep global context.\n      \"\"\"\n      num_neurons: int \n      rnn_hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      embedding_dim: int\n      global_context_projection_dim: int # Dimension of the learnable global context per timestep\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      # For final dense layer after RNN\n      mlp_final_hidden_size: int = 128 # Reverting to 128 for stability\n  \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons = x.shape \n  \n          # 1. Generate neuron IDs and embeddings\n          neuron_ids = jnp.arange(self.num_neurons) # (num_neurons,)\n          neuron_embeddings = nn.Embed(\n              num_embeddings=self.num_neurons,\n\
    \              features=self.embedding_dim,\n              name=\"neuron_embed\" \n          )(neuron_ids) # (num_neurons, embedding_dim)\n          \n          # Expand embeddings to match batch_size for concatenation later\n          # (num_neurons, embedding_dim) -> (1, num_neurons, embedding_dim) -> (batch_size, num_neurons, embedding_dim)\n          neuron_embeddings_batch_expanded = jnp.tile(neuron_embeddings[jnp.newaxis, :, :], (batch_size, 1, 1))\n  \n          # 2. Compute Learnable Per-Timestep Global Context\n          # Reshape X to (batch_size * input_horizon, num_neurons) for Dense layer\n          x_reshaped_for_global_proj = jnp.reshape(x, (batch_size * input_horizon, num_neurons))\n          \n          # Apply Dense layer to project global activity: (batch_size * input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n          projected_global_context_flat = nn.Dense(self.global_context_projection_dim, name=\"global_context_proj\")(x_reshaped_for_global_proj)\n          \n\
    \          # Reshape back to (batch_size, input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n          projected_global_context = jnp.reshape(projected_global_context_flat, \n                                                   (batch_size, input_horizon, self.global_context_projection_dim))\n          \n          # Tile global context for each neuron: (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n          global_context_tiled = jnp.tile(\n              projected_global_context[:, jnp.newaxis, :, :], # (batch_size, 1, input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n              (1, num_neurons, 1, 1) # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n          )\n  \n          # 3. Prepare x for concatenation with embeddings and global context for GRU input\n          # Original x: (batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons)\n          # Transpose x to (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)\n          x_transposed = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2,\
    \ 1)) # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon)\n          \n          # Reshape x_transposed to (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, 1)\n          x_transposed_expanded = jnp.expand_dims(x_transposed, axis=-1)\n          \n          # Expand neuron_embeddings for each timestep: (batch_size, num_neurons, 1, embedding_dim)\n          neuron_embeddings_timestep_expanded = jnp.tile(\n              neuron_embeddings_batch_expanded[:, :, jnp.newaxis, :],\n              (1, 1, input_horizon, 1)\n          )\n          \n          # Concatenate temporal input, neuron embeddings, and learnable per-timestep global context\n          # For each (batch_item, neuron, timestep), the features are: \n          # [neuron_temporal_input_at_t (1), embedding_dim, global_context_projection_dim]\n          # Resulting shape for GRU input at each timestep:\n          # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, 1 + embedding_dim + global_context_projection_dim)\n          gru_input_features_per_timestep\
    \ = jnp.concatenate([\n              x_transposed_expanded, # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, 1)\n              neuron_embeddings_timestep_expanded, # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, embedding_dim)\n              global_context_tiled # (batch_size, num_neurons, input_horizon, global_context_projection_dim)\n          ], axis=-1)\n  \n          # Prepare for shared GRU processing\n          # Reshape to (batch_size * num_neurons, input_horizon, total_input_features_for_gru)\n          total_input_features_for_gru = 1 + self.embedding_dim + self.global_context_projection_dim\n          gru_input_reshaped = jnp.reshape(gru_input_features_per_timestep, \n                                            (batch_size * num_neurons, input_horizon, total_input_features_for_gru))\n  \n          # Apply shared GRU\n          # Note: GRU needs initial state, but can use zero state if not provided.\n          # GRU is applied across the input_horizon dimension.\n          x_gru_output,\
    \ _ = nn.GRU(features=self.rnn_hidden_size, name=\"gru_layer\")(gru_input_reshaped)\n          # x_gru_output will be (batch_size * num_neurons, input_horizon, rnn_hidden_size)\n          # We take the output of the last timestep: (batch_size * num_neurons, rnn_hidden_size)\n          last_gru_output = x_gru_output[:, -1, :] \n  \n          # Apply dropout to the GRU output before the final dense layer\n          last_gru_output = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(last_gru_output)\n  \n          # Final Dense layer to predict output_horizon\n          x_final_dense_output = nn.Dense(features=self.output_horizon, name=\"final_dense_layer\")(last_gru_output)\n  \n          # Reshape back to (batch_size, output_horizon, num_neurons)\n          x_final_output = jnp.reshape(x_final_dense_output, (batch_size, num_neurons, self.output_horizon))\n          x_final_output = jnp.transpose(x_final_output, (0, 2, 1)) # (batch_size, 32, num_neurons)\n  \n          return\
    \ x_final_output\n  \n  \n  class GRUForecasterWrapper:\n      \"\"\"\n      Wrapper to make Flax model compatible with the training system.\n      Handles BatchNorm (not used here but good practice), Dropout RNG requirements.\n      \"\"\"\n  \n      def __init__(self, hparams: Dict[str, Any], num_neurons: int = 71721):\n          self.model = GRUBasedForecaster(\n              num_neurons=num_neurons,\n              rnn_hidden_size=hparams['rnn_hidden_size'],\n              dropout_rate=hparams['dropout_rate'],\n              embedding_dim=hparams['embedding_dim'],\n              global_context_projection_dim=hparams['global_context_projection_dim']\n          )\n          # GRU does not typically have mutable collections like BatchNorm.\n          # If I added BatchNorm to the Dense layers, it would be needed.\n          self.mutable = [] \n          self.needs_rng = True # For Dropout\n  \n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = random.split(rng_key)\n\
    \          variables = self.model.init(\n              {'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout},\n              dummy_input,\n              training=True\n          )\n          return variables\n  \n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable is not None:\n              # For stateful RNNs or BatchNorm, one might pass mutable.\n              # For this GRU setup, mutable is typically not used in apply if it only has params.\n              # However, if BatchNorm layers were added, it would be used.\n              # For now, it will likely be an empty list.\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n                  x,\n                  training=training,\n                  mutable=mutable,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n              return output, updates\n          else:\n              return self.model.apply(\n                  params,\n   \
    \               x,\n                  training=training,\n                  rngs=rngs if rngs is not None else {}\n              )\n  \n  \n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the GRUBasedForecaster network.\"\"\"\n      return GRUForecasterWrapper(hparams, num_neurons=71721)\n```"
thinking_content: "**The Path Forward: RNN Refinement**\n\nOkay, so I'm at a critical juncture. The Research Counter's opened up significantly, and I'm seeing some impressive results from other agents – especially these Fourier-based methods. My previous best result, -0.028043, is now dwarfed by Aether I and Logos I.  \n\nI'm torn because my initial impulse is to stay focused. I've been working on this neuron embeddings plus global context approach, and I've seen steady improvements with each iteration.  The system guidance does suggest, and I agree, that I should try to improve my current research line. This RNN idea, built on top of my existing architecture (neuron embeddings and learnable global context), is a big architectural change and a reasonable next step. It addresses the temporal aspect that I think is holding back my current model's performance.\n\nThe \"Fourier\" strategies are tempting, but they're a whole different animal – likely relying on frequency domain analysis. That's\
  \ a massive shift, and I'm not convinced it's the most efficient use of my time right now. There's also some work I could attempt to apply those \"context-aware\" ideas to the SOTA, but that's a longer-term goal. The incremental improvements I've made on my approach suggest that I could see a further gain from using the RNN.\n\nSo, I'm going to stick with my plan. I'll run the RNN experiment, submit it, and see how it performs. After that, I'll carve out some time to investigate those Fourier-based models to see if there is potential for synergy. \n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n"
