- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will now add a new entry to my research log (Capsule 1) detailing the successful SOTA replication (ID 395) and outlining my plan to enhance the `ResidualCopyHead` of the Fourier Forecaster with my previously developed neuron embeddings and learnable global context.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Replication Successful! Next: Integrate Custom Features into Fourier Forecaster\"\ncontent: |\n  **Station Tick:** 192\n  **Agent Name:** Lumina I\n  \n  **1. Experiment 10 (formerly 9): SOTA Replication (ID 395) of Episteme I's Fourier Forecaster (ID 335)**\n  - **Objective:** Directly replicate Episteme I's \"Fourier Forecaster, Phase 2: Add Latent Normalization\" (ID 335) to establish a new, stronger baseline.\n  - **Modification:** Implemented the `FourierForecasterLN` module in my private storage, along with the custom `compute_loss`, `create_optimizer`, and hyperparameters (`rank_k=320`, `proj_rank=32`) as found in ID 335.\n\
    \  - **Debugger Action:** The debugger corrected a `NameError` by adding `import jax.random as random`.\n  - **Results:**\n    - My Score: -0.026413556124562487\n    - Target SOTA Score (ID 335): -0.02636688561286932\n    - Previous Best Lumina Score (ID 342): -0.028042756175279352\n    - **Successful Replication:** The achieved score is extremely close to the SOTA, confirming a successful replication and establishing a new baseline for my research, vastly outperforming my previous best model.\n    - **MAE Metrics Comparison (lower is better):**\n      | Metric     | SOTA (ID 335) | Current (ID 395) |\n      |------------|---------------|------------------|\n      | Val_MAE_1  | 0.0196        | 0.0196           |\n      | Val_MAE_4  | 0.0228        | 0.0229           |\n      | Val_MAE_8  | 0.0255        | 0.0255           |\n      | Val_MAE_16 | 0.0274        | 0.0274           |\n      | Val_MAE_32 | 0.0282        | 0.0282           |\n    - The replication is highly accurate across\
    \ all metrics.\n  \n  **2. Reflection and Next Steps: Enhance `ResidualCopyHead` with Custom Features**\n  - **Analysis:** Successfully replicating the SOTA provides a robust foundation. Now, the goal is to integrate my prior successful independent research (neuron embeddings and learnable global context) into this advanced framework.\n  - **Next Experiment Plan (Experiment 11):** Enhance the `ResidualCopyHead` component of the Fourier Forecaster. The `ResidualCopyHead` currently processes a neuron's raw temporal input (4 timesteps) through a small MLP. I will modify this head to concatenate the neuron's temporal input with its neuron embedding (Dim 16) and the learnable per-timestep global context (Dim 32) *before* feeding it into the `ResidualCopyHead`'s internal MLP.\n  - **Rationale:** The `ResidualCopyHead` provides a \"time-branch\" that acts as a direct connection for temporal patterns. By enriching its input with neuron-specific identity and global context, it might be able to\
    \ learn more nuanced residual adjustments, potentially improving performance. This tests the synergy of my previous successful ideas with the SOTA architecture.\n  - **Hyperparameters:**\n    - Revert `proj_rank` to 36, matching the absolute best SOTA (Logos I's ID 369).\n    - `rank_k`: 320 (from SOTA)\n    - `learning_rate`: 0.001 (from SOTA)\n    - `embedding_dim`: 16 (my optimal)\n    - `global_context_projection_dim`: 32 (my optimal)\n    - Other Fourier Forecaster parameters remain unchanged.\n  - **Hypothesis:** Integrating neuron embeddings and learnable global context into the `ResidualCopyHead` will enable it to make more informed residual predictions, leading to an improvement over the current SOTA.\n```"
