    \    - Target SOTA Score (Aether I's ID 400): -0.026272\n    - My Previous SOTA Replication (ID 395): -0.026414\n    - **Successful Re-Replication:** The achieved score is extremely close to the current SOTA, successfully re-establishing a reliable baseline.\n    - **MAE Metrics Comparison (lower is better):**\n      | Metric     | SOTA (ID 400) | Current (ID 413) |\n      |------------|---------------|------------------|\n      | Val_MAE_1  | 0.0196        | 0.0196           |\n      | Val_MAE_4  | 0.0229        | 0.0228           | # Slight improvement!\n      | Val_MAE_8  | 0.0254        | 0.0255           | # Slight worsening\n      | Val_MAE_16 | 0.0272        | 0.0274           | # Slight worsening\n      | Val_MAE_32 | 0.0281        | 0.0282           | # Slight worsening\n    - The overall score is very close, confirming the stability of the baseline.\n  \n  **2. Reflection and Next Steps: Integrate Custom Features into Fourier Branch MLP**\n  - **Analysis of Previous Failure\
    \ (ID 405):** Directly integrating my custom features (neuron embeddings and learnable global context) into the `ResidualCopyHead` was detrimental. This suggests that the time-branch's simple MLP might not be capable of processing the expanded feature space effectively or that the specific inductive bias of the `ResidualCopyHead` (copying initial values) is sensitive to such changes.\n  - **New Integration Strategy:** Instead of enhancing the `ResidualCopyHead`, I will now focus on enriching the *Fourier Branch*. Specifically, I will concatenate the neuron embeddings (Dim 16) and the learnable per-timestep global context (Dim 32) directly with the `factors_in_flat` input to the *deep MLP within the Fourier Branch*.\n  - **Rationale:** The Fourier Branch is responsible for transforming input frequencies into predicted output frequencies, and its deep MLP is a central computational hub. By providing neuron-specific identity and global context at this stage, the MLP can learn how these\
    \ features modulate the frequency-domain factor predictions, potentially leading to a more profound synergy than with the simpler time branch. This allows the model to learn context-aware representations in the frequency domain itself.\n  - **Hyperparameters:**\n    - `rank_k`: 320 (from SOTA)\n    - `proj_rank`: 36 (from SOTA ID 400)\n    - `learning_rate`: 0.001 (from SOTA)\n    - `embedding_dim`: 16 (my optimal)\n    - `global_context_projection_dim`: 32 (my optimal)\n    - Other Fourier Forecaster parameters remain unchanged.\n  - **Hypothesis:** Integrating neuron embeddings and learnable global context into the Fourier Branch's latent factor MLP will enable the model to learn richer, context-modulated frequency-domain representations, leading to an improvement over the current SOTA.\n```"
