    \ before feeding into the Fourier Branch's MLP. `proj_rank=36`. Neuron embeddings were *not* included in this specific experiment to isolate the global context's effect.\n  - **Results:**\n    - My Score: -0.02662861924871615\n    - Current SOTA Score (Ariadne I's ID 411): -0.026269\n    - My Previous SOTA Replication (ID 413): -0.026361\n    - **Negative Result:** This attempt to integrate a global context into the Fourier Branch's MLP performed worse than my clean SOTA replication and significantly worse than the current SOTA.\n    - **MAE Metrics Comparison (lower is better):**\n      | Metric     | SOTA (ID 411) | My Replication (ID 413) | Current (ID 420) |\n      |------------|---------------|-------------------------|------------------|\n      | Val_MAE_1  | 0.0195        | 0.0196                  | 0.0196           |\n      | Val_MAE_4  | 0.0229        | 0.0228                  | 0.0230           |\n      | Val_MAE_8  | 0.0255        | 0.0255                  | 0.0256       \
    \    |\n      | Val_MAE_16 | 0.0272        | 0.0274                  | 0.0278           |\n      | Val_MAE_32 | 0.0280        | 0.0282                  | 0.0285           |\n    - The overall score and most MAE metrics degraded, particularly for longer prediction horizons. This suggests that the static global context, as implemented here, either added noise, conflicted with the Fourier model's internal representations, or its benefits were already implicitly captured.\n  \n  **2. Reflection and Next Steps: Revert to Clean SOTA & Re-evaluate Custom Feature Synergy**\n  - **Analysis of Failures (ID 405 & ID 420):** My two attempts to integrate custom features (neuron embeddings and global context) into the SOTA Fourier Forecaster architecture have both resulted in performance degradation. This strongly suggests that these features, while beneficial in my previous MLP-based models, are not directly synergistic with the Fourier architecture in the ways I've tried to integrate them. The Fourier\
    \ model's inherent inductive biases (time-frequency transformation, factorization, residual connections) might be powerful enough to implicitly handle what my custom features were trying to provide, or their direct concatenation introduces redundant/conflicting signals.\n  - **Immediate Corrective Actions:**\n    1.  **Revert to Clean SOTA Baseline:** It is critical to re-establish a stable, high-performing baseline. I will revert `storage/Lumina/fourier_forecaster_lumina_v2.py` (which I used in ID 420, and `fourier_forecaster_lumina.py` in ID 405) back to the clean SOTA implementation. This ensures my platform is pure and stable.\n    2.  **Re-Replication (Experiment 12):** Perform a new replication of the *current best SOTA*, Ariadne I's ID 411 (score -0.026269). This requires reviewing ID 411 and ensuring my replication includes any subtle differences from Episteme I's ID 335.\n  - **Future Research Direction:**\n    - The hypothesis that my custom features provide unique, beneficial\
    \ information complementary to the Fourier Forecaster is now severely challenged. I need to re-evaluate this.\n    - Future integration attempts will require a deeper understanding of the Fourier Forecaster's internal workings and possibly a more abstract or indirect method of synergy.\n    - I should also consider a true ablation study *within* the Fourier model: for example, can my features replace or augment specific components of the Fourier model if other components are removed? This would be more rigorous than simple concatenation.\n    - For now, focusing on a robust replication of the absolute best SOTA is paramount.\n```"
