thinking_content: '**Exciting Progress and Future Directions**


  Okay, here''s what I''m thinking. Experiment 443 just wrapped up, and the results are incredibly promising. The model with a learnable gamma ramp significantly outperformed the current SOTA, Ariadne I''s ID 411, with a score of -0.026125 compared to -0.026269. Even my own replication of the SOTA, ID 431, couldn''t beat it. This is a clear improvement and, depending on the overall leaderboard, might be a new SOTA!


  Looking at the per-timestep MAE metrics, there''s a slight trade-off in Val_MAE_1, but the improvements in later timesteps, especially Val_MAE_16 and 32, contribute to the better overall score. It''s a solid advancement!


  Given these results and the prompt''s encouragement to publish regularly, now is the perfect time to write a paper. I''m at tick 209, and this result is ready for a formal publication.


  My next immediate steps are clear: first, I''ll head to the Private Memory Room and update capsule #1 to document this breakthrough, highlighting the benefits of the learnable gamma. Then, I''ll prepare to publish this new SOTA paper in the Archive Room.


  As for future experiments, I have a few ideas. The most obvious is an ablation study on the gamma ramp itself, comparing different fixed and learnable gamma settings. I could also fine-tune hyperparameters around this new SOTA baseline, similar to what Ariadne I is doing with `hidden_size`.


  Finally, and this is a bit more tentative, I might revisit integrating my neuron embeddings/global context. But I need to approach this cautiously, focusing on integration that synergizes with Fourier processing, not disrupts it. For now, it''s more pragmatic to refine the existing architecture.


  My plan for next turn is straightforward: go to Private Memory and update Capsule 1. It''s time to document this exciting result and start planning the paper.

  '
