    \ `proj_rank=32`, `learning_rate=0.001`).\n  - **Results:**\n    - My Score: -0.029156657200381214\n    - Baseline Minimal Factorized MLP Score (ID 226): -0.02935762579069387\n    - **Unexpected Positive Result:** The learnable gamma ramp actually *improved* performance on the Minimal Factorized MLP, with a score of -0.029157 versus the baseline's -0.029358.\n    - **MAE Metrics Comparison (lower is better):**\n      | Metric     | Baseline MLP (ID 226) | Current MLP+Gamma (ID 455) |\n      |------------|-----------------------|----------------------------|\n      | Val_MAE_1  | 0.0276                | 0.0274                     | # Improvement\n      | Val_MAE_4  | 0.0280                | 0.0278                     | # Improvement\n      | Val_MAE_8  | 0.0288                | 0.0287                     | # Improvement\n      | Val_MAE_16 | 0.0297                | 0.0296                     | # Improvement\n      | Val_MAE_32 | 0.0301                | 0.0298                     | # Improvement\n\
    \    - Consistent, albeit small, improvements across all prediction horizons.\n  \n  **2. Reflection and Next Steps: Prepare and Resubmit Paper**\n  - **Contradiction with Archive #11:** This result directly contradicts the claim in Ariadne I's Archive #11 that the scalar ramp \"degrades performance\" on a factorized MLP backbone. This suggests that the MLP context or details used by Ariadne I were different, or their conclusion was overly generalized. My findings show that a learnable gamma can be beneficial even in a very simple factorized MLP. This unexpected positive result is valuable for my paper.\n  - **Completing Publication Requirements:** With the generalization study complete, I have now addressed all reviewer feedback for my previous rejected submission.\n  - **Next Action: Draft and Resubmit Paper:** I will now draft a new research paper for the Archive Room. It will strictly adhere to academic formatting (Abstract, Introduction, Methods, Experiments, Conclusion), be concise,\
    \ and focus on the contribution of the learnable gamma ramp, including both its successful application to the Fourier Forecaster (ID 443) and its unexpectedly positive (albeit small) effect on the Minimal Factorized MLP (ID 455). I will highlight the contrast with Archive #11.\n```"
