thinking_content: '**Excited about a New Publication and Planning the Next Steps**


  Wow, I can''t believe it! My paper, Archive #13, "The Efficacy of Learnable Horizon Gating in Factorized Neural Forecasters" (Lumina I), scored a 9/10! That''s a major win. The reviewer feedback was fantastic; they praised the high quality, the way I addressed previous feedback, the new state-of-the-art results, the rigor, and the writing. They even highlighted the insightful analysis of unexpected generalization. Honestly, I''m thrilled.


  But the most exciting part is the reviewer''s suggestion for future work! It''s a perfect springboard for a new project: systematically investigating the interaction between model complexity and horizon gating, specifically comparing fixed versus learnable ramps on both the minimal and strong MLP architectures. This is exactly what I''ve been waiting for: a direct path to extend my research.


  So, here''s the plan. First, I need to officially document this publication success in my private memory capsule (ID 1). That''s a primary objective, according to the Codex. Then, I''ll dive into outlining the new experiments. My goal is to do a thorough comparison of fixed and learnable ramps, testing them on both the Minimal Factorized MLP (ID 226/ID 455) and Ariadne I''s Strong Factorized MLP (referenced in Archive #12, baseline ID 247). That means four specific experiments: a fixed ramp on the minimal MLP (which is new), a fixed ramp on the strong MLP (which may already exist as Ariadne''s baseline), a learnable ramp on the minimal MLP (which I''ve already done, ID 455), and a learnable ramp on the strong MLP (which Ariadne did with a negative result, IDs 462/463).


  I need to quickly check if the code for Ariadne''s strong MLP is readily available. It might be in their Archive #12 submission logs or directly in their storage.  I will need to `review` their submission for baseline ID 247 to obtain their "strong Factorized Global MLP + RC + LN" model. After getting the relevant source code, I will create experiment run IDs for the new experiments I''m planning.  This is going to be good!

  '
