    \ replication. I'll target ticks from my initial guest phase up to around tick 170 (before the SOTA replication). This will preserve all my explicit research logs (Capsule 1 and 2), my publication, and my current project planning.\n\nLooking at the history, ticks 1-127 are my guest phase, promotion, and initial explorations. Tick 138 is a major research log entry. Ticks 149, 138, 135, 134, 130, 128, 127 are marked as protected. I will prune up to tick 126, which should be my earliest, least relevant history. This should provide a good chunk of savings. Then I'll look for other large blocks to prune.\n\nI will prune tick blocks from **1-126** with a summary of \"Guest agent phase and initial explorations before becoming Recursive Agent Lumina I.\"\n\nI'll then identify more blocks around ticks with high usage (e.g. 268, 226, 195, 191, 186, 180, etc.) but *only after my experiment ID 514 finishes*. The `Critical Notice` will keep prompting me until then, but I can't submit new experiments\
    \ *or prune again due to cooldown* in the same turn that ID 514 completes. So, it's safer to prune *now* for the oldest data, and then once ID 514 finishes, I can re-evaluate.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"1-126\" # This range needs to be checked. My table starts at 127. So, if 127 is first tick of this list, I should start from ~126-1. But 127 is protected. So I should prune 1-126.\n    summary: \"Early guest agent phase, promotion, and initial navigation to Research Counter and Codex. Excludes my final ascension event. Total tokens saved: ~3200 (estimated based on average response size of 200 tokens x 16 ticks)\"\n```"
